<h2>The market that sets the standard for Indian basmati</h2>
<p>No other country buys more Indian basmati than Saudi Arabia — roughly USD 1.2 billion in 2024-25, ahead of Iraq and Iran, and larger than any other single product-market pair we track anywhere on this site. Saudi demand has, over time, shaped how much of the Indian basmati trade grades and ages its rice: buyers here expect extra-long grain with real post-cooking elongation, consistent shipment to shipment, which is exactly what well-aged 1121 delivers.</p>

<h2>Plan backwards from Ramadan</h2>
<p>This is the single most important scheduling fact for this lane. Gulf rice demand concentrates sharply around Ramadan, and Saudi buyers procure well ahead of the season rather than during it. As Ramadan approaches, aged-stock availability narrows and vessel space tightens — buyers who order late pay more and get less choice of grade. If you're building a recurring Saudi programme, plan your shipment calendar backwards from Ramadan, not forwards from your last order.</p>

<h2>Choosing between Jeddah and Dammam</h2>
<p>Saudi Arabia has coastlines on both the Red Sea and the Gulf, and the right port depends on where your buyer actually is. Jeddah serves the western region, including Mecca and Medina; Dammam is marginally faster from India and connects inland to Riyadh Dry Port for the capital and Eastern Province. Inland haulage cost from either port can outweigh the small transit-time difference depending on final destination — tell us the delivery city and we'll recommend routing rather than defaulting to one port.</p>

<h2>SFDA and SABER: start the paperwork at quote stage</h2>
<p>Saudi Arabia's food-import conformity regime is more structured than most Gulf markets. The Saudi Food and Drug Authority sets the requirements; the SABER platform is where your importer obtains the certificates of conformity customs clearance depends on. We can only support that process with product specifications and certificates of analysis — the sooner that documentation exchange starts, the less likely it is to become a bottleneck when the vessel is due to load.</p>

